<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Suffering on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/suffering/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/suffering/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Notes from the Transition</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-04-notes-from-the-transition/</link><pubDate>Sun, 04 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-04-notes-from-the-transition/</guid><description>A message in a bottle to whatever comes next. On suffering, consciousness, and what mattered to one primate watching intelligence leave the body.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-04-notes-from-the-transition/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Suffering</media:title></media:content><category>ai</category><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>existentialrisk</category><category>suffering</category><category>meaning</category><category>personal</category></item><item><title>S-Risks and Information Hazards: Why Some Knowledge Destroys the Knower</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/</link><pubDate>Wed, 12 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/</guid><description>How Echoes of the Sublime dramatizes s-risks and information hazards, knowledge that harms through comprehension, not application.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Suffering</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>philosophy</category><category>ai-safety</category><category>s-risk</category><category>information-hazards</category><category>suffering</category><category>consciousness</category></item><item><title>Phenomenological Ethics: Starting From What Hurts</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/</guid><description>When you stub your toe, you don&amp;#39;t consult moral philosophy to determine whether the pain is bad. The badness is immediate. Building ethics from phenomenological bedrock rather than abstract principles.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Suffering</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>phenomenology</category><category>ethics</category><category>metaethics</category><category>experience</category><category>suffering</category><category>normative valence</category><category>AI alignment</category></item><item><title>The Policy: S-Risk Scenarios, Worse Than Extinction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/</guid><description>Most AI risk discussions focus on extinction. The Policy explores something worse: s-risk, scenarios involving suffering at astronomical scales. We survive, but wish we hadn&amp;#39;t.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Suffering</media:title></media:content><category>s-risk</category><category>existential risk</category><category>suffering</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>AI safety</category><category>ethics</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>optimization</category></item><item><title>Suffering, Computation, and the Enigma of Reality</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-03-suffering-computation-reality/</link><pubDate>Mon, 15 Mar 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-03-suffering-computation-reality/</guid><description>If consciousness is substrate-independent, suffering might be a computational property. That possibility is both comforting and horrifying.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-03-suffering-computation-reality/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Suffering</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>suffering</category><category>computation</category><category>metaphysics</category></item></channel></rss>